"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Fellows, Ladies and Gentlemen, at a historical juncture characterised by breathtaking technological acceleration and perpetual cultural turnover, a quiet yet consequential fracture is widening between generations: the crisis of intergenerational transmission. For millennia, human civilisations endured because each generation accepted the dual responsibility of preserving hard-won cultural, linguistic, ecological, and civic wisdom from its predecessors while passing it on, critically renewed, to its successors. Today, caught between a youth culture conditioned to treat anything older than yesterday's algorithm as obsolete and an older generation increasingly uncertain of what deserves to be handed down, modern societies risk drifting into an impoverished eternal present severed from both memory and foresight.",
"In her seminal essay 'The Crisis in Education', Hannah Arendt illuminated the profound ethical stakes of this rupture: education and cultural transmission are fundamentally acts of love for the world and for newcomers. When adults abdicate their responsibility to introduce the young to the shared historical world—out of a misguided desire to leave children entirely to their own spontaneous devices—they do not liberate youth; rather, they abandon them to the tyranny of immediate peer conformity and commercial fads.",
"Long before the digital age, Walter Benjamin warned in 'The Storyteller' that the rise of fragmented, rapidly consumed information was eroding the ancient art of narrative wisdom—the capacity to weave lived experience, moral counsel, and historical memory into stories that endow human existence with continuity. In an era of ephemeral feeds and twenty-four-hour news cycles, collective amnesia ceases to be an accidental lapse and becomes a structural feature of everyday life.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the educational orthodoxy with which certain modern reformers dismiss deep historical literacy, classical languages, and canonical literature as 'elitist baggage' ill-suited to the digital economy—while simultaneously lamenting that young citizens struggle to recognise authoritarian propaganda, historical revisionism, or ecological tipping points whose roots stretch back centuries.",
"Equally perilous, however, is the opposite distortion: turning heritage into a lifeless museum relic or a weapon of exclusionary nostalgia. As the French philosopher Paul Ricœur reminded us in 'Memory, History, Forgetting', living tradition is never mechanical repetition; it is a dynamic dialogue between inherited debt and critical reinterpretation, in which each generation honours its ancestors precisely by questioning, translating, and expanding their legacy.",
"To speak of a crisis of transmission is therefore not, Ladies and Gentlemen, to advocate an authoritarian pedagogy of rote memorisation or to silence the legitimate rebellion of youth against inherited injustices. Every generation must retain the sovereign right to challenge past blind spots and invent new forms of beauty and justice.",
"Therefore, before this assembly, I call for a renewed covenant between generations: restoring the dignity of public schools, libraries, and universities as sanctuaries of slow, cumulative inquiry; creating civic forums where elders and young citizens collaborate directly on ecological restoration and local memory; and embracing the timeless insight that a civilisation only possesses a future to the extent that it remains capable of conversing critically and gratefully with its past."
],
