"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Delegates, Ladies and Gentlemen, if future historians seek a single phrase to characterise the opening decades of the twenty-first century, they may well describe ours as an age of permanent systemic uncertainty. Where post-war social democracies once built civic solidarity upon assumptions of steady economic growth, predictable career trajectories, and climatic stability, contemporary societies must now navigate overlapping disruptions—ecological destabilisation, rapid artificial intelligence transformation, demographic shifts, and geopolitical volatility. Before this assembly, a defining question confronts every democratic polity: when inherited certainties dissolve, will human societies fracture into fearful enclaves of mutual suspicion, or can we forge a deeper, more resilient architecture of social cohesion rooted in shared vulnerability?",
"In 'A Theory of Justice', the philosopher John Rawls offered an enduring moral compass for societies confronting radical unpredictability: behind a 'veil of ignorance' that conceals whether we will be born affluent or impoverished, healthy or vulnerable, employed or displaced by technological upheaval, rational citizens would design institutions that protect the least advantaged among us. In an era when climate shocks and algorithmic automation can rapidly overturn anyone's security, Rawlsian solidarity ceases to be abstract philanthropy and becomes the essential foundation of collective resilience.",
"The philosopher Martha Nussbaum has enriched this insight through her 'capabilities approach', arguing that genuine social cohesion cannot rest upon cold economic aggregates alone; it requires guaranteeing every citizen the concrete material, educational, health, and civic capabilities needed to live with dignity and participate meaningfully in public life. Where millions feel abandoned to precarious labour, failing public services, and cultural contempt, demagogues effortlessly exploit their anxiety by turning neighbours against newcomers.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the short-sightedness with which certain champions of market deregulation dismantle public healthcare, affordable housing, and universal social insurance in the name of 'fiscal agility', only to express bewildered alarm when social trust collapses, political extremism surges, and civic solidarity evaporates into tribal resentment.",
"Conversely, history warns us against seeking cohesion through forced ideological unanimity or exclusionary nationalism. As the economist and philosopher Amartya Sen has shown throughout his work on democracy and public reason, durable social trust in pluralistic societies does not require citizens to share identical beliefs or origins; it flourishes when diverse people possess fair institutional channels to debate disagreements peacefully and see their voices reflected in collective decisions.",
"Building cohesion under conditions of permanent uncertainty does not mean, Ladies and Gentlemen, promising citizens an impossible return to a static, risk-free world—such promises are the stock-in-trade of authoritarian illusionists. Rather, it means replacing fragile certainty with shared democratic agency.",
"Therefore, before this assembly, I call for a twenty-first-century social covenant tailored to an age of turbulence: establishing universal, portable social protections that shield every human being across life transitions and technological shifts; investing boldly in common civic institutions—public schools, libraries, universal healthcare, and ecological commons—where citizens of all backgrounds experience mutual dependence firsthand; and cultivating an ethic of civic friendship capable of transforming our shared vulnerability from a source of fear into the bedrock of a just and enduring human community."
