    "texte": [
      "Convening at the University of Oxford for a public symposium on cross-cultural ethics, the social anthropologist Professor Henrik Lindholm and the moral philosopher Dr Amina Osei engaged in a probing debate over whether universal human values can withstand the challenge of moral relativism. 'For more than a century, empirical anthropology has demonstrated that human societies organise kinship, justice, property, and sacred duty according to remarkably diverse moral grammars,' Professor Lindholm began. 'Whenever powerful Western states have proclaimed their own historical norms to be \"universal truths\", those claims have all too frequently served as moral camouflage for imperial conquest, cultural erasure, and paternalistic intervention.'",
      "'You expose the historical abuse of pseudo-universalism with undeniable force, Henrik, yet moral relativism collapses the moment we confront grave systemic cruelty,' Dr Osei replied with measured precision. 'If every cultural code is equally immune to external moral judgement simply because it is locally customary, then outsiders possess no principled ground on which to condemn chattel slavery, torture, the subjugation of women, or the persecution of minorities—and worse still, internal reformers and dissidents within those very societies are betrayed as cultural traitors.'",
      "'I do not suggest that anthropologists must applaud cruelty, Amina, but rather that moral judgements only gain persuasive traction when articulated from within a society's own evolving traditions rather than imposed from above by foreign tribunals,' Lindholm countered, refining his stance. 'Imposing abstract human rights declarations onto communities without engaging their local moral vocabulary often provokes defensive backlash rather than genuine ethical transformation.'",
      "'It is particularly wry to observe the selective relativism practised by certain authoritarian rulers on the international stage,' Osei remarked with a dry smile: 'they eagerly import Western surveillance technologies, luxury automobiles, and global financial instruments without the slightest qualm about \"cultural authenticity\", yet the instant citizens demand free elections, an independent judiciary, or freedom of the press, those rights are suddenly denounced as incompatible Western imports.'",
      "'That cynical double standard is indeed indefensible,' Lindholm conceded warmly. 'To critique imperial hypocrisy is never to grant tyrants a cultural licence to oppress their own populations—every living culture contains internal voices of dissent, compassion, and justice struggling against entrenched power.'",
      "'Precisely!' Osei agreed, welcoming the synthesis. 'As both Isaiah Berlin's \"value pluralism\" and Martha Nussbaum's \"capabilities approach\" demonstrate, we do not have to choose between uniform cultural imperialism and nihilistic relativism: there exists a shared core of fundamental human capabilities—bodily integrity, freedom of conscience, political participation, and shelter from humiliation—that every culture can honour through its own distinct institutional and poetic forms.'",
      "Henrik Lindholm nodded in agreement: 'In short, universal values survive the test of relativism not as a monolithic Western monologue imposed upon the world, but as an ongoing, egalitarian dialogue in which diverse traditions hold one another accountable to the irreducible dignity of every human being.'"
