"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Jurists and Scholars, Ladies and Gentlemen, when the drafters of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights proclaimed in 1948 that all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights, they forged an ethical cornerstone for the modern world. Yet across contemporary political and philosophical debates, an insidious temptation repeatedly resurfaces: the attempt to sever 'human dignity' from 'individual freedom'. On one side, paternalistic authoritarian regimes claim to protect their citizens' material security, social harmony, or cultural dignity while stripping away freedom of conscience, speech, and political choice; on the other side, radical market libertarians celebrate an abstract freedom of contract while remaining indifferent to the humiliating poverty, homelessness, and exploitation that crush human dignity. Before this assembly, a foundational question demands our lucid judgement: can genuine human dignity ever survive when divorced from individual freedom?",
"In his 'Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals', Immanuel Kant established the philosophical bond uniting dignity and freedom with unmatched rigor: unlike commercial commodities that possess a relative 'price' and can be exchanged for an equivalent, a human being possesses an unconditional inner worth—'dignity'—precisely because we are rational, autonomous moral agents capable of legislating moral laws for ourselves. To treat a human being merely as a passive instrument to be managed, indoctrinated, or coerced 'for their own good' is to violate their dignity at its very root.",
"Equally profound, however, is the complementary insight developed by Amartya Sen and Martha Nussbaum: formal legal freedom rings hollow when a person is deprived of the material, educational, and social capabilities required to exercise real agency. A citizen sleeping under a bridge or trapped in bonded labour possesses nominal constitutional liberty on paper, yet systemic destitution strips away the concrete autonomy through which human dignity is lived.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the symmetrical sophistry deployed by authoritarian paternalists and hyper-capitalist apologists alike: the autocrat boasts of lifting citizens out of poverty while imprisoning anyone who dares to question state policy, as though well-fed obedience were indistinguishable from human dignity; meanwhile, the market fundamentalist applauds the 'sacred freedom' of a desperate worker to accept starvation wages, as though economic coercion were the triumph of liberty.",
"Historical memory confirms that whenever dignity and freedom are pitted against one another as rival priorities, both perish together. Regimes that abolish political liberty in the name of collective dignity inevitably degenerate into unaccountable oligarchies that humiliate the very citizens they claimed to protect; conversely, societies that exalt unregulated individual liberty while abandoning social solidarity fracture into oligarchic inequality where the many are subjugated to the unchecked power of the few.",
"To insist upon the indivisibility of dignity and freedom does not mean, Ladies and Gentlemen, ignoring difficult practical tensions—such as balancing free expression against protection from hate speech, or reconciling individual property rights with ecological stewardship. Democracy exists precisely to adjudicate such tensions openly under the rule of law.",
"Therefore, before this assembly, I call upon jurists, educators, and citizens to defend the unbroken unity of human dignity and individual freedom as the twin pillars of any civilisation worthy of the name: guaranteeing inviolable civil and political liberties that honour every person's moral autonomy, securing universal social, educational, and ecological rights that empower every individual to stand upright without humiliation, and affirming that humanity reaches its true stature only when every human being is recognised simultaneously as an end in themselves and a free co-author of our common world."
