    "texte": [
      "For nearly three decades following the dissolution of the Soviet Union in 1991, Western strategic orthodoxy rested upon a comforting teleology: the belief that international politics had settled permanently into a single liberal order anchored by American primacy, globalised free trade, and universal institutional norms. Today, that unipolar certitude has visibly fractured. From the rapid industrial and technological ascent of East and South Asia to the assertive diplomacy of middle powers across Africa, Latin America, and the Middle East, the international system is undergoing a tectonic redistribution of wealth, demographic weight, and normative influence. Yet the central question confronting contemporary statecraft is whether this fragmentation of the post-Cold War order will degenerate into an anarchic clash of rival spheres of influence, or give birth to a genuinely pluralistic and balanced international society.",
      "In his classic study 'The Anarchical Society', the international relations theorist Hedley Bull drew a vital distinction between a mere 'international system'—in which states interact solely through raw calculations of power and fear—and an 'international society', which emerges only when sovereign states recognise shared interests and bind themselves to common rules, diplomatic conventions, and institutions that restrain arbitrary violence. As new powers legitimately demand an end to Western institutional monopolies forged in 1945, the urgent challenge is to renegotiate those shared rules rather than allow international society to relapse into a Hobbesian state of nature.",
      "The political scientist Graham Allison famously warned of the 'Thucydides Trap'—the structural danger that arises whenever a rising power threatens to displace an established hegemon, generating mutual suspicion and escalatory miscalculation. Yet reducing twenty-first-century geopolitics to a binary duel between two superpowers gravely misreads the breadth of the contemporary transformation: what is emerging today is not a neat two-bloc Cold War replay, but what the scholar Amitav Acharya has termed a 'multiplex world'—a decentralised order populated by multiple regional centres of gravity, diverse civilisational traditions, and agile middle powers refusing automatic alignment.",
      "It is particularly wry to observe the selective indignation with which established powers denounce 'revisionist challenges to the rules-based international order' whenever rising states seek greater voting weight in global financial institutions, while quietly bypassing those very same rules through unilateral tariffs, extraterritorial sanctions, or selective military interventions whenever international law proves inconvenient to their own strategic interests.",
      "At the same time, the emergence of new regional powers offers no automatic guarantee of global justice or peaceful cooperation. History repeatedly shows that rising states can rapidly replicate the very imperial arrogance, resource extractivism, and spheres-of-influence coercion they once condemned when speaking from the periphery.",
      "To diagnose the fragmentation of the post-1945 architecture is therefore neither to mourn the passing of Western hegemony nor to celebrate a lawless multipolarity in which might makes right. A fragmented world devoid of enforceable norms invariably punishes smaller nations and imperils the planetary commons.",
      "Forging a stable, equitable order out of contemporary fragmentation requires replacing unilateral hegemony with inclusive institutional reform: overhauling the United Nations Security Council, the Bretton Woods financial institutions, and global trade rules so they reflect twenty-first-century demographic and economic realities; cultivating habits of strategic empathy and conflict-prevention diplomacy among rival powers; and anchoring international legitimacy not in the dominance of any single civilisation, but in the shared survival and equal dignity of all peoples upon a fragile Earth."
