"texte": [
"For much of the late twentieth century, liberal internationalism rested upon an article of faith that seemed almost self-evident: the deeper the web of cross-border trade, financial integration, and supply-chain interdependence binding nations together, the costlier and lessthinkable geopolitical coercion would become. Commerce, in Montesquieu's famous formulation, was supposed to soften manners and pacify rivalries. Yet in the opening decades of the twenty-first century, statecraft has turned that comforting doctrine upside down: rather than preventing coercion, dense economic interdependence has itself become the primary battlefield of geopolitical power, as states weaponise financial clearinghouses, semiconductor chokepoints, energy pipelines, and export controls to compel adversaries without firing a shot.",
"The political scientists Henry Farrell and Abraham Newman have named this structural mutation 'weaponised interdependence'. Because globalised networks of finance, digital communication, and high-technology manufacturing are rarely symmetrical—instead concentrating critical hubs within specific jurisdictions—states controlling those central nodes can exploit 'panopticon' surveillance effects to monitor global transactions and 'chokepoint' effects to sever targeted nations from vital credit, software, or microchips.",
"More than a century ago, in 'The Great Illusion', Norman Angell argued that modern economic entanglement rendered military conquest economically self-defeating even for the victor. Today, coercive economic diplomacy encounters an analogous boomerang paradox: when powerful states deploy sweeping financial sanctions or export bans against major economies, they rarely achieve swift political capitulation; instead, they inflict collateral inflation upon their own consumers, disrupt global supply chains, and inadvertently incentivise rival powers to build parallel payment systems and autonomous industrial ecosystems.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the strategic surprise expressed by policy planners in Washington or Brussels when targeted states fail to collapse on schedule under comprehensive sanctions packages—as though sovereign nations subjected to economic siege would meekly overthrow their own governments on cue rather than rallying nationalist resentment around the very leaders targeted by foreign pressure.",
"Furthermore, indiscriminate economic coercion frequently inflicts its harshest toll not upon insulated political elites—who readily circumvent restrictions through shell companies and black-market intermediaries—but upon ordinary civilians deprived of imported medicines, agricultural fertilisers, and stable livelihoods.",
"To scrutinise the limits and unintended blowback of economic coercion is not, of course, to claim that democracies must remain passive in the face of military aggression, human rights atrocities, or nuclear proliferation. Targeted, multilaterally coordinated measures remain an indispensable alternative to catastrophic armed conflict.",
"Exercising coercive diplomacy wisely in an era of deep interdependence therefore demands strategic restraint, legal precision, and humbler expectations: replacing sweeping, unilateral economic blockades with narrowly targeted sanctions aimed strictly at culpable leaders and military supply chains, safeguarding humanitarian exemptions for food and medicine, and recognising that durable geopolitical influence rests far less on threatening chokepoints than on offering reliable, mutually beneficial partnerships."
