"texte": [
"Convening at the Graduate Institute in Geneva for a public debate on twenty-first-century governance, the constitutional jurist Professor Martin Castellano and the international political economist Dr Priya Subramanian confronted a defining transformation of modern statehood: can traditional national sovereignty survive the ascent of transnational actors whose wealth, infrastructural reach, and cross-border mobility rival or surpass those of medium-sized states? 'Since the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 and Jean Bodin's classical theory of indivisible sovereignty, democratic self-government has presupposed that a territorial state exercises supreme authority over its laws, currency, taxation, and public sphere,' Professor Castellano began. 'Today, global digital platforms govern public speech across continents, multinational corporations shift profits effortlessly across tax havens, and transnational asset managers wield decisive leverage over sovereign bond markets—hollowing out democratic sovereignty from within.'",
"'You diagnose the erosion of Westphalian territorial authority with compelling clarity, Martin, yet we should beware of treating national sovereignty as an immutable relic that can simply be sealed behind nineteenth-century borders,' Dr Subramanian replied with measured nuance. 'As the sociologist Saskia Sassen has demonstrated in her studies of global cities and transnational governance, sovereignty has not merely vanished; it has been partially disassembled and reconfigured across cross-border networks that enable climate cooperation, scientific research, and global supply chains.'",
