"texte": [
"Long before the invention of cyber-sabotage or semiconductor embargoes, the Athenian decree barring Megarian merchants from every port of the Delian League demonstrated that trade restrictions could serve as a lethal prelude—or substitute—for military assault. Yet in the twenty-first century, economic warfare has evolved from an occasional wartime siege tactic into a permanent, peacetime instrument of geopolitical domination. Through extraterritorial financial penalties, sovereign asset freezes, technology blacklists, and maritime insurance blockades, powerful states now seek to bend adversaries to their will by severing their lifelines to the global economy.",
"In his classic 1945 study 'National Power and the Structure of Foreign Trade', the economist Albert Hirschman uncovered the silent geopolitical leverage hidden inside asymmetric trade relations: whenever a large, diversified economy becomes the indispensable market or supplier for a smaller state, trade ceases to be a neutral exchange of mutual benefit and becomes a potent instrument of political coercion—because the cost of severing the relationship is trivial for the dominant partner yet catastrophic for the dependent state.",
"The historian Nicholas Mulder has traced in 'The Economic Weapon' how modern sanctions emerged after the First World War as an ostensibly humane alternative to trench warfare—a bloodless deterrent intended to enforce collective security through the threat of commercial isolation. Over time, however, as economic coercion migrated from rare multilateral League of Nations mandates to unilateral decrees enforced through the supremacy of the US dollar and Western clearing systems, the 'economic weapon' shed its collective legitimacy and became an instrument of unilateral primacy.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the legalistic acrobatics by which dominant financial powers justify 'extraterritorial secondary sanctions': a sovereign state declares that a company in a third country—operating entirely outside the sanctioning state's territory and violating none of its own national laws—will be expelled from global banking networks unless it obeys foreign geopolitical decrees.",
