"texte": [
"Mr President, Excellencies, Distinguished Ambassadors, Ladies and Gentlemen, built from the ashes of two catastrophic world wars in 1945, the modern multilateral system embodied humanity's most audacious institutional wager: that sovereign nations could replace the law of the jungle with collective deliberation, binding international treaties, and shared responsibility for peace and human dignity. Eighty years later, that noble architecture stands visibly strained to the breaking point. From deadlocked Security Council resolutions over devastating armed conflicts to stalled climate finance negotiations and vaccine nationalism during global pandemics, multilateralism appears trapped in an acute structural contradiction: entrusted with solving planetary crises that no single state can master alone, international institutions remain hostage to the narrow short-term national interests of the very member states that created them.",
"In 'Perpetual Peace', Immanuel Kant anticipated this fundamental tension with philosophical clarity: sovereign states behave in international affairs much like individuals in a lawless state of nature, clinging jealously to unrestricted freedom of action even when that very anarchy exposes every nation to mutual ruin. Kant recognised that a durable federation of free states cannot rely upon sentimental goodwill alone; it requires binding legal covenants that align each nation's enlightened self-interest with the preservation of universal peace.",
"The political scientist John Ruggie illuminated the post-war compromise that made twentieth-century multilateralism viable under the concept of 'embedded liberalism': international institutions succeeded when they balanced open multilateral cooperation with legitimate domestic policy space allowing governments to protect social welfare and employment. When hyper-globalisation eroded domestic social protections while leaving global governance institutions unrepresentative of the Global South, public trust in multilateralism began to wither from within.",
"It is particularly wry to observe the diplomatic ritual performed annually at global summits: heads of state take the podium to proclaim with solemn emotion that 'climate change, pandemics, and nuclear proliferation know no borders' and demand 'urgent collective action'—before flying home to veto binding emissions targets, hoard critical medical patents, or invoke national security exceptions to paralyse international dispute tribunals.",
